  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct human L1 gold records for nucleoside analogs consistently show absolute oral bioavailability above 20%: didanosine 22–43% (C04, C09, C10, C15, C16), lamivudine 69–87% (C05, C07, C17, C19), zalcitabine 80% (C18), zidovudine 61% (C06), stavudine 61–78% (C11), viramidine 66.1% (C08), and controlled-release ddI 26–35% (C14, C21).",
      "card_ids": ["C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C13","C14","C15","C16","C17","C18","C19","C21"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 records add supportive values: lamivudine 93% and 86% (C25, C26), zalcitabine 30% in fasted mice (C31), and viramidine 43.9% in monkeys (C30). The only sub-20% value is viramidine in rats (9.91%) despite 61.7% oral absorption (C24), indicating species-specific first-pass metabolism rather than a general absorption failure.",
      "card_ids": ["C25","C26","C30","C31","C24"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 C58 shows didanosine rat intestinal L→B permeability of 1.4×10^-6 cm/s with 98% recovery; despite this low permeability, didanosine human F is 22–43%, so low permeability/efflux mechanisms do not by themselves establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C58","C04","C09","C10","C15","C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records for related molecules show low intrinsic clearance or lack of major CYP metabolism: AZT glucuronidation CLint ~0.7–3.8 µL/min/mg (C69, C70, C72, C73, C74, C75, C77, C80), GS-441524 not a major CYP substrate (C76, C78), dapagliflozin CYP oxidation <10% (C79), and low theophylline metabolite formation in rat microsomes (C71). None of these provide affirmative evidence for high first-pass loss in the query, and no query-specific high-clearance record exists.",
      "card_ids": ["C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79","C80"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77","C78","C79","C80"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "The query's N-amino ribosyl linkage is not represented in any L1/L2/L3/L4/L5/L6 context; acid stability, transporter affinity, and first-pass behavior are unmeasured.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic-stability data are available; L6 records are for other molecules (AZT, GS-441524, dapagliflozin, theophylline) with low transferability.",
    "No human data exist for a xanthine N-aminoriboside of this type.",
    "The only sub-20% F value in the evidence base is viramidine in rats (C24), which is species-specific and not transferable to human oral bioavailability."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because direct human L1 gold records for nucleoside analogs all exceed 20% (didanosine 22–43%, lamivudine 69–87%, zalcitabine 80%, zidovudine 61%, stavudine 61–78%, viramidine 66.1%). L2 adds supportive values; the only sub-20% value is viramidine in rats (9.91%) with 61.7% absorption, indicating species-specific first-pass metabolism. L6 hepatic-clearance records show low intrinsic clearance / lack of major CYP metabolism for related molecules and provide no affirmative evidence for F<20%; no query-specific high-clearance record exists. Confidence remains moderate."
}

